From: mscharged-port
Subject: Bind shadow volume arrays through their actual byte domains
Upstream-base: f75ccf1ad20e5bd7585962990db08067c0ae498a

The original stadium shadow volumes count faces in EFB alpha: front faces add
4/255, back faces subtract it, and the blend pass darkens every pixel whose
count stays above zero. In the game module GXShadowVolume still declared all of
its arrays little-endian, a contract of the earlier preview readers. The stadium
volume models come from the original resource loader and keep Wii big-endian
floats, so their positions decoded to values around 1e37. Volumes then became
open, randomly placed polygons whose counts did not cancel, and the blend pass
darkened large hard-edged regions of the field that changed with the camera.

Bind the three arrays through SetGameGraphicsArray like every other material
program: loaded data stays Wii-serialized (big-endian), native writer output is
native-endian. GLShadowBlendMeshWriter publishes its written native streams as
the other mesh writers do, so the blend quad resolves through the same path.
Original vertex formats, counts, strides, raster state and blend arithmetic are
unchanged; preview builds keep their explicit native arrays.
diff --git a/src/Game/GL/GLShadowBlendMeshWriter.cpp b/src/Game/GL/GLShadowBlendMeshWriter.cpp
--- a/src/Game/GL/GLShadowBlendMeshWriter.cpp
+++ b/src/Game/GL/GLShadowBlendMeshWriter.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,9 @@
